      "caption": "f(x) = 3x − 6 and its inverse are reflections of each other in the line y = x"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Inverse of $y = ax^2$"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The inverse of $y = ax^2$ is $y = \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{x}{a}}$, which is **not a function** (one $x$ gives two $y$-values). To make the inverse a function, **restrict the domain** of $f$, e.g. to $x \\geq 0$."
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example 2",
      "problem": "Given $f(x) = 2x^2$, $x \\geq 0$. Find $f^{-1}$ and state its domain.",
      "steps": [
        "Swap: $x = 2y^2$, so $y^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$",
        "$y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{x}{2}}$ (positive root, because $y \\geq 0$)",
        "Domain of $f^{-1}$: $x \\geq 0$"
      ],
      "answer": "$f^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}$, $x \\geq 0$"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Inverse of $y = b^x$: the logarithm"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The inverse of $y = b^x$ is $x = b^y$, which is written $y = \\log_b x$. It is defined only for $x > 0$, and its graph has the $y$-axis as a vertical asymptote."
    },
